{
  "version": 1,
  "id": "00000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000101",
  "title": "Mandelbrot Set",
  "created_at": "2026-01-01T00:00:00Z",
  "updated_at": "2026-01-01T00:00:00Z",
  "shared_cargo_toml": "[profile.release]\nopt-level = 1\nlto = false\ncodegen-units = 16",
  "cells": [
    {
      "id": "cell_0",
      "order": 0,
      "label": "Introduction",
      "cell_type": "Markdown",
      "source": "# Mandelbrot Set 🌀\n\nThe **Mandelbrot set** is the most famous fractal in mathematics. It is defined as the set of complex numbers $c$ for which the iteration\n\n$$z_{n+1} = z_n^2 + c, \\quad z_0 = 0$$\n\nremains bounded ($|z| \\le 2$) as $n \\to \\infty$.\n\n## The Escape-Time Algorithm\n\nFor each pixel we map its position to a complex number $c$, then iterate the formula above. If $|z|$ exceeds 2, the sequence will diverge — the number of iterations before escape determines the pixel's color. Points that never escape (up to our iteration limit) are colored black — these lie *inside* the Mandelbrot set.\n\nThe boundary is a fractal of infinite complexity: no matter how far you zoom in, there is always more detail.\n\n## Multi-Core Execution ⚡\n\nThis notebook uses [rayon](https://docs.rs/rayon) to compute pixels across all available CPU cores in parallel. Each row of the image is distributed to a separate thread via `par_iter`, providing near-linear speedup on multi-core machines.",
      "cargo_toml": null
    },
    {
      "id": "cell_1",
      "order": 1,
      "label": "Full Mandelbrot View",
      "cell_type": "Code",
      "source": "use ironpad_cell::prelude::*;\n\nlet (width, height) = (800u32, 600u32);\nlet max_iter = 100u32;\n\n// Classic full view: x ∈ [-2.5, 1.0], y ∈ [-1.25, 1.25]\nlet (x_min, x_max) = (-2.5_f64, 1.0);\nlet (y_min, y_max) = (-1.25_f64, 1.25);\n\n// Bernstein-polynomial color map: black interior,\n// smooth blue → teal → orange gradient outside\nfn color_map(n: u32, max_n: u32) -> (u8, u8, u8) {\n    if n == max_n {\n        return (0, 0, 0);\n    }\n    let t = n as f64 / max_n as f64;\n    (\n        (9.0 * (1.0 - t) * t * t * t * 255.0) as u8,\n        (15.0 * (1.0 - t) * (1.0 - t) * t * t * 255.0) as u8,\n        (8.5 * (1.0 - t) * (1.0 - t) * (1.0 - t) * t * 255.0) as u8,\n    )\n}\n\n// Compute all pixels in parallel using rayon.\nlet pixels: Vec<(u8, u8, u8)> = (0..height)\n    .into_par_iter()\n    .flat_map(|y| {\n        (0..width).map(move |x| {\n            let cx = x_min + (x as f64 + 0.5) / width as f64 * (x_max - x_min);\n            let cy = y_min + (y as f64 + 0.5) / height as f64 * (y_max - y_min);\n\n            let (mut zx, mut zy) = (0.0_f64, 0.0_f64);\n            let mut n = 0u32;\n            while zx * zx + zy * zy <= 4.0 && n < max_iter {\n                let tmp = zx * zx - zy * zy + cx;\n                zy = 2.0 * zx * zy + cy;\n                zx = tmp;\n                n += 1;\n            }\n\n            color_map(n, max_iter)\n        })\n    })\n    .collect();\n\nlet canvas = Canvas::from_pixels(width, height, pixels);\n\nCellOutput::from(canvas)\n",
      "cargo_toml": "[dependencies]\nrayon = \"1\""
    },
    {
      "id": "cell_2",
      "order": 2,
      "label": "Seahorse Valley Zoom",
      "cell_type": "Code",
      "source": "use ironpad_cell::prelude::*;\n\nlet (width, height) = (800u32, 600u32);\nlet max_iter = 200u32; // Higher limit reveals finer detail\n\n// Seahorse valley — a region of beautiful spirals\nlet (x_min, x_max) = (-0.77_f64, -0.73);\nlet (y_min, y_max) = (0.08_f64, 0.12);\n\nfn color_map(n: u32, max_n: u32) -> (u8, u8, u8) {\n    if n == max_n {\n        return (0, 0, 0);\n    }\n    let t = n as f64 / max_n as f64;\n    (\n        (9.0 * (1.0 - t) * t * t * t * 255.0) as u8,\n        (15.0 * (1.0 - t) * (1.0 - t) * t * t * 255.0) as u8,\n        (8.5 * (1.0 - t) * (1.0 - t) * (1.0 - t) * t * 255.0) as u8,\n    )\n}\n\n// Compute all pixels in parallel using rayon.\nlet pixels: Vec<(u8, u8, u8)> = (0..height)\n    .into_par_iter()\n    .flat_map(|y| {\n        (0..width).map(move |x| {\n            let cx = x_min + (x as f64 + 0.5) / width as f64 * (x_max - x_min);\n            let cy = y_min + (y as f64 + 0.5) / height as f64 * (y_max - y_min);\n\n            let (mut zx, mut zy) = (0.0_f64, 0.0_f64);\n            let mut n = 0u32;\n            while zx * zx + zy * zy <= 4.0 && n < max_iter {\n                let tmp = zx * zx - zy * zy + cx;\n                zy = 2.0 * zx * zy + cy;\n                zx = tmp;\n                n += 1;\n            }\n\n            color_map(n, max_iter)\n        })\n    })\n    .collect();\n\nlet canvas = Canvas::from_pixels(width, height, pixels);\n\nCellOutput::from(canvas)\n",
      "cargo_toml": "[dependencies]\nrayon = \"1\""
    },
    {
      "id": "cell_3",
      "order": 3,
      "label": "Explore Further",
      "cell_type": "Markdown",
      "source": "## ◉ Explore Further\n\nTry modifying the zoom parameters to explore other interesting regions:\n\n| Region | x range | y range |\n|--------|---------|----------|\n| Elephant Valley | \\[0.25, 0.40\\] | \\[−0.10, 0.10\\] |\n| Spiral | \\[−0.088, −0.086\\] | \\[0.654, 0.656\\] |\n| Mini-brot | \\[−1.790, −1.780\\] | \\[−0.005, 0.005\\] |\n| Lightning | \\[−1.26, −1.24\\] | \\[0.04, 0.06\\] |\n\nIncrease `max_iter` for deeper zooms to reveal finer detail. The fractal is infinitely deep — you are limited only by floating-point precision!",
      "cargo_toml": null
    }
  ],
  "description": "Zoom into the infinite complexity of the Mandelbrot fractal rendered as a color-mapped SVG.",
